A||||1||William Federle||Mechical temp gage hookup||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||05-05-2000||08:58 PM||||Sometime ago, someone posted a reply that showed a picture of a mechanical temp guage through the back window of their Fiero. They had it hooked up to check the Fiero guage. Can anyone point me to that thread?<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||05-09-2000||07:56 PM||||Well, I didn't get any answers on that one so I went ahead and got a "mechanical" temp gage from the Fleet Farm store. My fan switch doesn't work anyway (at least I've never seen it work) so I bought a 23MM socket and pulled it and screwed the temp gage bulb into that hole in the block.<P>I learned something very interesting about my temperature gage. I thought it was badly out of wack because it seemed to be at 190 deg or so at the first 1/4 gage mark. It turns out that that is 190 deg F. and the 220 deg mark in the middle is just about right too. I let the temp go up to slightly more than 200 deg. before I chickened out, and the Fiero gage was just below the 220 mark when my mechanical gage was at its 200 mark. I had thought that the 1/4 scale mark on the fiero gage should be about 160 (100 + 1/2 of 120). I think Pontiac should have spent the extra 50 cents/car and marked the 1/4 scale mark.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 05-09-2000).]||||reg||1
